"5190", "journal_title": "Phys.Rev.Lett."}}}, {"reference": {"publication_info": {"journal_volume": "18", "artid": "756", "page_start": "756", "journal_title": "J.Math.Phys."}}, "curated_relation": false, "record": {"$ref": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/109298"}}, {"reference": {"publication_info": {"journal_volume": "332", "artid": "145", "page_start": "145", "journal_title": "Chem.Phys.Lett."}}}, {"reference": {"publication_info": {"journal_volume": "20", "artid": "1743", "page_start": "1743", "journal_title": "J.Phys.A"}}}, {"reference": {"publication_info": {"journal_volume": "48", "artid": "1808", "page_start": "1808", "journal_title": "Phys.Rev.A"}}}], "number_of_pages": 10, "referenced_authors_bais": ["R.Tumulka.1", "D.Durr.1", "Nino.Zanghi.1", "S.Goldstein.1", "K.Rheinberger.1", "John.Stewart.Bell.1", "D.Bohm.1", "B.Misra.1", "E.C.G.Sudarshan.1", "G.Gruebl.1"], "figures": [{"filename": "crletf1neu.png", "material": "preprint", "caption": "Two patterns of world lines as they may arise from some Bell-type QFT. (a)~The world line of a photon (dashed curve) starts at an emission event (at time $t_1$) on the world line of an electron (bold curve), and ends at an absorption event (at time $t_2$) on the world line of another electron. (b)~An electron--positron pair (bold curves) is created at the end point of a photon world line.", "label": "figone", "source": "arxiv", "key": "4bfc990550dab61874b0fbff560033d4", "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/4bfc990550dab61874b0fbff560033d4"}, {"filename": "crletf2.png", "material": "preprint", "caption": "Schematic representation of the configuration space of a variable number of particles. (a)--(d) show the sectors $\\Q^{[0]}$ through $\\Q^{[3]}$. A configurational history $Q(t)$ jumps to the next higher sector at each creation event, and to the next lower sector at each annihilation event. The history shown corresponds to a world-line pattern like that of figure \\ref{figone}(a).", "label": "figtwo", "source": "arxiv", "key": "2d8f02af60edb74e8b999ad5278f5aa0", "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/2d8f02af60edb74e8b999ad5278f5aa0"}], "legacy_version": "20190508164757.0", "inspire_categories": [{"term": "General Physics"}, {"term": "Quantum Physics", "source": "arxiv"}], "legacy_creation_date": "2003-03-27", "preprint_date": "2003-03", "author_count": 4, "public_notes": [{"source": "arXiv", "value": "4 pages, uses RevTeX4, 2 figures; v2: shortened and with minor additions"}], "first_author": {"full_name": "Duerr, Detlef", "last_name": "Duerr", "first_name": "Detlef", "recid": 1045354}, "control_number": 615844, "dois": [{"value": "10.1103/PhysRevLett.93.090402"}, {"material": "publication", "source": "arXiv", "value": "10.1103/PhysRevLett.93.090402"}], "earliest_date": "2003-03", "document_type": ["article"], "texkeys": ["Duerr:2003erc", "Durr:2003gu"], "abstracts": [{"value": "We discuss a recently proposed extension of Bohmian mechanics to quantum field theory. For more or less any regularized quantum field theory there is a corresponding theory of particle motion, which in particular ascribes trajectories to the electrons or whatever sort of particles the quantum field theory is about. Corresponding to the nonconservation of the particle number operator in the quantum field theory, the theory describes explicit creation and annihilation events: the world lines for the particles can begin and end. These theories also provide a definition (and facilitate the probabilistic prediction) of the time of creation."}, {"source": "arXiv", "value": "We discuss a recently proposed extension of Bohmian mechanics to quantum field theory. For more or less any regularized quantum field theory there is a corresponding theory of particle motion, which in particular ascribes trajectories to the electrons or whatever sort of particles the quantum field theory is about. Corresponding to the nonconservation of the particle number operator in the quantum field theory, the theory describes explicit creation and annihilation events: the world lines for the particles can begin and end.", "abstract_source_suggest": {"input": "arXiv"}}], "refereed": true, "primary_arxiv_category": ["quant-ph"], "external_system_identifiers": [{"schema": "ADS", "value": "2004PhRvL..93i0402D"}, {"schema": "SPIRES", "value": "SPIRES-5508193"}, {"schema": "MSNET", "value": "2110548"}], "titles": [{"title": "Bohmian mechanics and quantum field theory"}, {"source": "arXiv", "title": "Bohmian mechanics and quantum field theory."}, {"source": "arXiv", "title": "Bohmian Mechanics and Quantum Field Theory"}], "facet_author_name": ["1039950_Roderich Tumulka", "1039952_Sheldon Goldstein", "1039953_Nino Zanghi", "1045354_Detlef Dürr"], "_oai": {"sets": ["Literature"], "id": "oai:inspirehep.net:615844", "updated": "2026-02-06T09:57:00.944431"}, "curated": true, "journal_title_variants": ["Phys. Rev. Lett.", "Phys.Rev.Lett."], "arxiv_eprints": [{"categories": ["quant-ph"], "value": "quant-ph/0303156"}]}, "updated": "2026-02-06T09:57:00.944431+00:00", "created": "2003-03-27T00:00:00+00:00"}], "total": 1}, "links": {"self": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.93.090402&size=5&page=1", "bibtex": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.93.090402&size=5&page=1&format=bibtex", "latex-eu": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.93.090402&size=5&page=1&format=latex-eu", "latex-us": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.93.090402&size=5&page=1&format=latex-us", "json": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.93.090402&size=5&page=1&format=json", "json-expanded": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.93.090402&size=5&page=1&format=json-expanded", "cv": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.93.090402&size=5&page=1&format=cv"}}